    "number": 50,
    "title": "Shading: PBR (Principled BSDF Shader)",
    "texts": [
      "Shading: PBR (Principled BSDF Shader)",
      "Shading",
      "PBR (Physically Based Rendering or Shading) is a computer graphics method that models how light behaves in the real world to create realistic and consistent materials.",
      "Diffuse Reflection",
      "Specular Reflection"
    ],
    "media": [
      "ppt/media/image91.png",
      "ppt/media/image92.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "number": 51,
    "title": "Shading: Metallics or Non-Metallics",
    "texts": [
      "Shading: Metallics or Non-Metallics",
      "Shading",
      "Metallics have no diffuse reflection (their diffusion is always black), their color is coming from specular reflection.",
      "Non-Metallics have strong diffuse reflection and carry only very weak specular reflection.",
      "In PBR however both diffuse and specular reflection are cominb from base color property.",
      "To simplify everything for you, the Metallic property should almost always be treated as a binary switch: 0 (non-metal/dielectric) or 1 (metal). This will tell shader how to interpret the base color corectly."
    ],
    "media": [
      "ppt/media/image93.png",
      "ppt/media/image94.png",
      "ppt/media/image95.jpg",
      "ppt/media/image96.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "number": 52,
    "title": "Shading: Roughness",
    "texts": [
      "Shading: Roughness",
      "Shading",
      "Every surface has micro-reliefs, such as pores or scratches. All of this causes the effect of surface roughness. If the surface is perfectly smooth, then the reflection is perfectly clear and distinct.",
      "Roughness Intensity"
    ],
    "media": [
      "ppt/media/image97.png",
      "ppt/media/image98.png",
      "ppt/media/image99.png",
      "ppt/media/image100.png",
      "ppt/media/image101.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "number": 53,
    "title": "Shading: Specular",
    "texts": [
      "Shading: Specular",
      "Shading",
      "As previously mentioned, non-metallics also have a weak specular reflection (around 0-8%). This information is not stored in the metallic map or base color and can be controlled using the specular map.",
      "Specular Intesity",
      "So don\u2019t use metallic property to control reflections of the Non-Metallic surfaces, use specular property instead."
    ],
    "media": [
      "ppt/media/image102.png",
      "ppt/media/image103.png",
